  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/aa872c37fc7912e4c246c80c391ee37ae57f23f6c1010681c367ce2ec6d901c2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(3)}$",
